 ", where h is a hadron, can be interpreted as hadron masses. The program can \
be used to derive mass sum rules by first generating amplitude sum rules and \
then reformatting the results in terms of masses.\n\nHere we generate sum \
rules for the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["\[CapitalOmega]", "B"], "-"], " ", "->", " ", 
    SuperscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["\[CapitalOmega]", "B"], "-"]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"368f7aac-340b-f342-bbcd-701f6ec53650"],
 " system. We view its amplitude table using the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"printAmps", "[", 
   RowBox[{"system", ",", "..."}], "]"}]], "InlineCode",ExpressionUUID->
  "56acb6f3-419d-3644-a6f8-e8001b6a2188"],
 " function.\n\nNote: The Hamiltonian for this system is a singlet (u = 0) \
that we input as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}]], "InlineCode",ExpressionUUID->
  "b4d62224-ba80-d64c-8555-86c1d523f6dd"],
 ". This is because we are using the ",
 StyleBox["physical",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " mode (which takes in the ",
 StyleBox["coefficients",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of the Hamiltonian) rather than the ",
 StyleBox["abstract",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " mode (which takes in the ",
 StyleBox["representation",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of the Hamiltonian)."
}], "Text",
